A complex river delta viewed from above in the style of Piet Mondrian, with water channels fragmenting into increasingly intricate geometric patterns of primary colors—bold red, yellow, and blue rectangles separated by thick black lines. The main river enters as a single wide blue channel at the top, then progressively splits into dozens of smaller perpendicular pathways, each intersection forming perfect right angles. Some channels dead-end into solid color blocks while others reconnect downstream. The composition suggests organized chaos, where apparent complexity emerges from simple repeated divisions. White spaces represent sandbars between the flowing geometric waterways, creating negative space that breathes between the rigid grid structure. The overall effect balances the tension between a single origin point and multiple diverging destinations, all contained within the strict neoplastic visual language of intersecting horizontal and vertical lines.
